    ],
  },
  {
    label: "State / Threads",
    cells: [
      "Depends on the client implementation",
      "Strong native support (threads, runs, persistence)",
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Best for",
    cells: [
      "Multi-model agents, open ecosystems, reusable tools",
      "Fast OpenAI-centric products and prototypes",
    ],
  },
];

const chooseMcp = [
  "You want tool portability across different models",
  "You are building multi-model or open-source agents",
  "You want a growing ecosystem of reusable tool servers",
  "Avoiding vendor lock-in is important",
];

const chooseAssistants = [
  "You are fully committed to the OpenAI stack",
  "You need fast development with built-in threads, file search, and code interpreter",
  "You want a managed runtime rather than assembling protocol + servers yourself",
];

const mcpWhy = [
  {
    title: "Before MCP: custom glue everywhere",
    body: "Every IDE, agent framework, and chat product invented its own way to register tools. A “GitHub integration” for one stack rarely worked in another without a rewrite.",
  },
  {
    title: "MCP’s job: a shared contract",
    body: "Model Context Protocol defines how hosts (agents, IDEs, chat apps) talk to servers that expose tools, resources, and prompts. Write a tool server once; connect it to multiple MCP-capable clients.",
  },
  {
    title: "Why that matters in 2026",
    body: "Teams run several agents (coding, ops, support). Rebuilding the same connectors three times is expensive and insecure. A protocol reduces duplicate work and concentrates security review on the server.",
  },
];

const interoperabilityPoints = [
  {
    title: "Portable tool servers",
    body: "A well-built MCP server for your internal API can serve Claude Code, Cursor, and a custom agent runtime — as long as each host speaks MCP.",
  },
  {
    title: "Clearer security boundary",
    body: "Tools live behind a server with its own auth, logging, and allowlists. The model proposes calls; the host and server still enforce policy.",
  },
  {
    title: "Faster ecosystem growth",
    body: "Open tool servers and community connectors multiply. Buyers should still verify quality — protocol support is not the same as production-grade tools.",
  },
  {
    title: "Less lock-in on the edge",
    body: "You can change agent hosts more easily if tools are not hard-wired into one vendor’s proprietary plugin format. Core product logic may still be vendor-specific.",
