---

<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  category="Technical"
  published={published}
  readTime={readTime}
  slug={slug}
  breadcrumbLabel="MCP & tool calling"
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Illustration of AI agents connecting to tools through a shared protocol"
  exploreCategories={["Frameworks", "Enterprise", "No-Code", "Coding"]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <!-- 1. Intro -->
  <section id="introduction">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Why tool calling and MCP matter for agent platforms</h2>
    <p>
      AI agent platforms only become useful when models can{" "}
      <strong>act</strong> — open tickets, query warehouses, edit repos, update
      CRM records — not merely chat. That capability rests on{" "}
      <strong>tool calling</strong> (also called function calling). In 2026 a
      second question matters just as much: will you rebuild those tools for
      every host, or share them through an open contract like{" "}
      <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong>?
    </p>
    <p>
      This guide is for technical founders, eng leads, and platform buyers who
      shortlist agent products. It explains the concepts in plain language, maps
      who supports MCP-style interoperability, and gives a practical evaluation
      checklist — without treating any protocol as a magic security feature. When
      you are ready to ship a server, use the companion{" "}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-server-implementation/">
        practical MCP server implementation guide
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <KeyTakeaway>
      <strong>Tool calling</strong> is the behavior that lets agents act.{" "}
      <strong>MCP</strong> is a shared plug shape so many hosts can reuse the
      same tool servers. Evaluate both: native tool quality for the jobs you
      need today, and protocol support if you will run multiple agents against
      the same internal systems — never confuse an MCP logo with IAM, DLP, or
      production-grade tools.
    </KeyTakeaway>
    <InfoCallout title="How to use this guide" variant="tip">
      Hold your real golden tasks constant (e.g. create ticket, query internal
      API, open PR). Score whether a platform’s tool model can complete them
      with traces and least privilege — then check whether MCP (or equivalent)
      would reduce duplicate connectors. Cross-check feature flags on{" "}
      <a href="/platforms/">All Platforms</a> and our{" "}
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>. To implement servers, see{" "}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-server-implementation/">
        How to Build and Run MCP Servers
      </a>
      .
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Tool calling -->
  <section id="tool-calling">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">What tool calling actually is</h2>
    <p>
      Large language models predict text. They do not magically open your CRM or
      deploy your app.{" "}
      <strong>Tool calling</strong> is the pattern where the model is allowed to
      request structured actions, and <em>your application</em> decides whether
      and how to run them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of the model as a coordinator with a menu of buttons. It can press
      “search knowledge base” or “create ticket,” but the buttons are wired by
      engineers with real credentials and guardrails. Without that loop, you
      have a chatbot. With it, you have an agent candidate.
    </p>

    <ol class="mt-8 !list-none !space-y-4 !p-0">
      {
        toolCallingSteps.map((s) => (
          <li class="flex gap-4 rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-border bg-surface-muted/40 p-5 sm:p-6">
            <span
              class="flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-[var(--radius-md)] bg-accent text-sm font-bold text-accent-fg"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {s.num}
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{s.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted sm:text-base">
                {s.body}
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <p>
      Computer-use (screen control) is a specialized form of acting on the
      world. Most business agents still rely primarily on API-style tools. Both
      need the same discipline: least privilege, logging, and tests.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. MCP vs traditional -->
  <section id="mcp-vs-tools">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">MCP vs traditional tool calling</h2>
    <p>
      Teams often conflate three different things: the model’s ability to
      request tools, an open protocol for hosting those tools, and a vendor’s
      proprietary connector catalog. Separating them prevents bad RFPs.
    </p>

    <ComparisonTable
      caption="Tool calling vs MCP vs proprietary connectors"
      headers={[
        "Traditional tool / function calling",
        "MCP (Model Context Protocol)",
        "Proprietary suite connectors",
      ]}
      rows={mcpVsToolsRows}
    />

    <p>
      In plain language: tool calling is the <em>behavior</em> (“the model asked
      to run a function”). MCP is a <em>shared plug shape</em> so many apps can
      use the same tool implementations. Enterprise suites often excel at
      connectors without exposing MCP at all — that can still be the right buy
      when the system of record is the product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 3b. MCP vs OpenAI Assistants -->
  <section id="mcp-vs-openai-assistants">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">MCP vs OpenAI Assistants</h2>
    <p>
      Buyers often compare MCP to the{" "}
      <strong>OpenAI Assistants API</strong> as if they were alternatives in
      the same product category. They solve different layers of the stack: MCP
      standardizes how tools and context plug into many hosts; Assistants is a
      managed OpenAI runtime for building assistants quickly. Choosing one does
      not always exclude the other.
    </p>

    <ComparisonTable
      caption="MCP vs OpenAI Assistants API"
      headers={["MCP (Model Context Protocol)", "OpenAI Assistants API"]}
      rows={mcpVsAssistantsRows}
    />

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card-surface p-6 sm:p-7">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-accent">When to choose MCP</h3>
        <ul class="mt-4 !list-none !space-y-3 !p-0">
          {
            chooseMcp.map((item) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                <span
                  class="mt-0.5 flex h-5 w-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-accent-soft text-[10px] font-bold text-accent"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                >
                  ✓
                </span>
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card-surface p-6 sm:p-7">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-accent">
          When to choose OpenAI Assistants
        </h3>
        <ul class="mt-4 !list-none !space-y-3 !p-0">
          {
            chooseAssistants.map((item) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                <span
                  class="mt-0.5 flex h-5 w-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-accent-soft text-[10px] font-bold text-accent"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                >
                  ✓
                </span>
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <InfoCallout title="They can work together" variant="info">
      Many teams now combine both approaches — using OpenAI’s runtime while
      connecting portable tools via MCP-style servers. Treat Assistants as the
      managed conversation/runtime layer and MCP (or equivalent) as how shared
      tools stay portable across hosts.
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. What MCP is -->
  <section id="what-is-mcp">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">
      What Model Context Protocol (MCP) is — and why it was created
    </h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> is an open standard for
      connecting AI applications (hosts) to external context and tools
      (servers). It was created to stop the explosion of one-off plugin formats
      as agents and coding assistants multiplied.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-8 !list-none !space-y-4 !p-0">
      {
        mcpWhy.map((item) => (
          <li class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{item.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted sm:text-base">
              {item.body}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Interoperability -->
  <section id="interoperability">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">
      How MCP improves interoperability between agents and tools
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
        interoperabilityPoints.map((p) => (
          <div class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{p.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{p.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Platform support -->
  <section id="platform-support">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">
      Which major platforms support MCP (or similar) in 2026
    </h2>
    <p>
      Support moves quickly. Treat this as a map of patterns, not a forever
      certification. Always confirm in current product docs before a purchase.
    </p>

    <ComparisonTable
      caption="MCP / tool-calling patterns by category (2026)"
      headers={["Examples", "Pattern", "Typical fit"]}
      rows={supportRows}
    />

    <InfoCallout title="Living shortlist in this directory" variant="tip">
      Filter platforms with the{" "}
      <a href="/platforms/">MCP feature chip</a> on All Platforms. Directory
      profiles flag MCP support on tools such as{" "}
      <a href="/tools/cursor/">Cursor</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/tools/claude-code/">Claude Code</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/tools/cline/">Cline</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/tools/langgraph/">LangGraph</a>, and{" "}
      <a href="/tools/anthropic-claude-agent-sdk/">Claude Agent SDK</a>.
    </InfoCallout>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
      {
        platformSupport.map((group) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-soft">
              {group.group}
            </h3>
            <ul class="mt-3 !list-none !space-y-3 !p-0">
              {group.items.map((item) => (
                <li class="card-surface p-5">
                  <a
                    href={item.href}
                    class="text-base font-semibold text-ink hover:text-accent"
                  >
                    {item.name}
                  </a>
                  <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                    {item.note}
                  </p>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Profiles -->
  <section id="profiles">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Platforms with strong MCP support</h2>
    <p>
      These directory profiles are useful starting points when MCP and
      tool-calling quality are selection criteria — especially for coding agents
      and open frameworks. Open each profile for scores, pricing, and feature
      flags.
    </p>

    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="cursor"
      rank={1}
      badge="MCP in the IDE"
      blurb="AI-native IDE with MCP tool integration — extend the daily coding agent with the same servers you use elsewhere."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="claude-code"
      rank={2}
      badge="CLI + MCP"
      blurb="Terminal-native Claude agent where MCP tools are a core extension path for repo-wide, tool-using sessions."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="cline"
      rank={3}
      badge="OSS + MCP"
      blurb="Open-source VS Code agent with plan/act and first-class MCP — max control; cost is mostly model APIs."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="anthropic-claude-agent-sdk"
      badge="Claude-native SDK"
      blurb="Build Claude-centric product agents with strong tool-use alignment in the ecosystem that drove MCP adoption."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="langgraph"
      badge="Framework + adapters"
      blurb="Own graphs, state, and tool policy in production — broad tool abstractions with maturing MCP bridges."
    />
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Implications -->
  <section id="implications">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Practical implications for buyers and builders</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {
        buyerImplications.map((col) => (
          <div class="card-surface p-6 sm:p-7">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-accent">{col.title}</h3>
            <ul class="mt-4 !list-none !space-y-3 !p-0">
              {col.points.map((point) => (
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                  <span
                    class="mt-0.5 flex h-5 w-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-accent-soft text-[10px] font-bold text-accent"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  >
                    ✓
                  </span>
                  <span>{point}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Limitations -->
  <section id="limitations">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Limitations and current maturity</h2>
    <InfoCallout title="MCP is not a security silver bullet" variant="warning">
      The protocol standardizes how tools are exposed. Misconfigured servers,
      over-broad tokens, and prompt injection against tool descriptions remain
      real risks. Always pair MCP with allowlists, least privilege, human
      approval for high-risk actions, and full tool-call traces.
    </InfoCallout>
    <ul class="mt-8 !list-none !space-y-4 !p-0">
      {
        limitations.map((item) => (
          <li class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{item.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted sm:text-base">
              {item.body}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. Evaluate -->
  <section id="evaluate">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">
      What to look for when evaluating tool / MCP support
    </h2>
    <p>
      Use this checklist in RFPs and technical spikes. A “yes” to MCP is never
      enough on its own.
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-8 !list-none !space-y-4 !p-0">
      {
        evalChecklist.map((item, i) => (
          <li class="flex gap-4 rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-border p-5 sm:p-6">
            <span
              class="flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-[var(--radius-md)] bg-accent-soft text-sm font-bold tabular-nums text-accent"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{item.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted sm:text-base">
                {item.body}
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <p>
      For a broader evaluation framework (autonomy, memory, guardrails, TCO, and
      more), see{" "}
      <a href="/guides/how-to-evaluate-ai-agent-platforms/">
        How to Evaluate AI Agent Platforms in 2026
      </a>
      . For category choice, see{" "}
      <a href="/guides/frameworks-vs-enterprise-platforms/">
        Frameworks vs Enterprise Platforms
      </a>
      . Coding-agent MCP surfaces are also covered in{" "}
      <a href="/guides/best-ai-coding-agents-2026/">Best AI Coding Agents 2026</a>{" "}
      and{" "}
      <a href="/guides/claude-code-vs-cursor/">Claude Code vs Cursor</a>. When you
      need to implement servers — tools, patterns, and production checklist —
      open the{" "}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-server-implementation/">
        MCP server implementation guide
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 11. Bottom line -->
  <section id="bottom-line">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Bottom line</h2>
    <p>
      Tool calling turns language models into systems that can act. MCP reduces
      how often you rebuild those actions for every agent host. Neither replaces
      product judgment: define the tools that matter, lock down permissions,
      measure outcomes, and pick platforms whose integration model matches how
      you actually ship software.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next: read the{" "}
      <a href="/guides/mcp-server-implementation/">
        practical guide to building and running MCP servers
      </a>
      , or open scored hosts with strong MCP stories —{" "}
